     I seem to be having a small problem with creating detatched 
processes and assigning devices for the detatched process to use
for I/O. My programs do as follows:

Parent: Calls SYS$GETDVI, asks for DVI$_DEVNAM (device name) No problem
          here, the terminal name comes back just fine. (Example "_TWA362")
        Build a descriptor to pass to SYS$CREPRC, using name passed
          back from $GETDVI and strlen() of that name. There might be a
          problem with the way I build the descriptor, but everything
          looks ok as far as I can tell.
        Call SYS$CREPRC using above descriptor as the _input_ parm, and 
          using the DETACH option. Again, no problem, the detached 
          process starts happily, no errors.

Child:  Builds descriptor for SYS$INPUT (using $DESCRIPTOR macro)
        Use SYS$ASSIGN to assign SYS$INPUT to a channel. So far, so good.
        Call SYS$GETDVI again, to make sure the child is dealing with
          the same device that the parent was. I pass the newly-assigned
          channel number to identify the device. Here is where things go
          south - $GETDVI returns a 1324, IVDEVAM error. Examining the
          value of the channel reveals the channel is 0 - that explains
          $GETDVI's problem, it thinks that I didn't pass it a channel
          or a device name to use. The question is, why is does the 
          $ASSIGN set the channel to 0?

     I am deeply confused. The parent process generally exits before
the child, but since the child is detached, is this a problem? I have
a suspistion the problem is with the parms to $CREPRC, but I can't
think of anything I'm doing wrong.

	I am a little new to VMS system programming, and it is entirel
possible I am missing some trivial detail. I would greatly appreciate
any help, and will happily provide code and more detail (if necessary)
to anybody. Responses by mail would be best, and I'll post a summary
if anything earthshattering comes out. Thanks, 

